{
  "id": "11wg891",
  "title": "Chapter 7: Electricity and Magnetism",
  "order": 56,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "eejwepu",
      "content": "Electricity and magnetism are closely related branches of physics. Electricity deals with the flow of electric charges, while magnetism deals with the magnetic properties of materials and magnetic fields produced by electric currents."
    },
    {
      "type": "statStrip",
      "stats": [
        {
          "label": "SI Unit of Current",
          "value": "Ampere (A)",
          "icon": "zap"
        },
        {
          "label": "SI Unit of Voltage",
          "value": "Volt (V)",
          "icon": "award"
        },
        {
          "label": "SI Unit of Resistance",
          "value": "Ohm (Ω)",
          "icon": "check"
        },
        {
          "label": "Key Relation",
          "value": "V = IR",
          "icon": "book"
        }
      ],
      "id": "98t1rp5"
    },
    {
      "id": "dvhjx1u",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Electricity"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Electric current is the rate of flow of electric charge through a conductor.",
        "Voltage (Potential Difference) provides the electrical energy required to push current through a circuit.",
        "Ohm's Law states that current is directly proportional to voltage and inversely proportional to resistance (V = IR).",
        "Magnets exert forces via magnetic fields, and electromagnets create controllable magnetic fields using electric current."
      ],
      "id": "kgkx7if"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "tbj49kn",
      "content": "Electricity is the flow of electric charge through a conductor. Electric current is the movement of electrons through a conductor such as copper wire."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "jjx3l15",
      "content": "• Electric charge is a fundamental property of matter."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "2h6y18j",
      "content": "• Electric current is the flow of electric charge."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ptrtpl8",
      "content": "• The SI unit of electric current is Ampere (A)."
    },
    {
      "id": "b0yjmmq",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2. Electric Current"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "0rv9qei",
      "content": "Electric current is defined as the rate of flow of electric charge."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "9rwtlze",
      "content": "Formula: I = Q / t"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "m00vhvm",
      "content": "Where:"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "v5nsytv",
      "content": "I = Electric current"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "bezs3s0",
      "content": "Q = Electric charge"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "33tjvc7",
      "content": "t = Time"
    },
    {
      "id": "tgg177u",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "3. Voltage"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "k1r79ny",
      "content": "Voltage is the potential difference between two points in an electric circuit."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "7gzqoei",
      "content": "SI Unit of Voltage = Volt (V)"
    },
    {
      "id": "3pb9zsk",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "4. Resistance"
    },
    {
      "type": "pullQuote",
      "content": "Electricity and Magnetism",
      "id": "9kfxsq6"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "kronuyj",
      "content": "Resistance is the opposition offered by a conductor to the flow of electric current."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "di3mxhs",
      "content": "SI Unit of Resistance = Ohm (Ω)"
    },
    {
      "id": "vaaacy4",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "5. Ohm’s Law"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "b61dnqa",
      "content": "Ohm’s Law states that the current flowing through a conductor is directly proportional to the voltage across it, provided temperature remains constant."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "vwqyulc",
      "content": "Formula: V = IR"
    },
    {
      "id": "um2d0ep",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "6. Electric Power"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ediscks",
      "content": "Electric power is the rate at which electrical energy is consumed."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "dy2bgcl",
      "content": "Formula: P = VI"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "1672380",
      "content": "SI Unit = Watt (W)"
    },
    {
      "id": "ctl2clf",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "7. Types of Electric Circuits"
    },
    {
      "type": "comparisonTable",
      "headers": [
        "Property",
        "Series Circuit",
        "Parallel Circuit"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Current (I)",
          "Same through each component",
          "Splits across different branches"
        ],
        [
          "Voltage (V)",
          "Divided across components",
          "Same across all parallel branches"
        ],
        [
          "Total Resistance",
          "Increases (R = R1 + R2 + ...)",
          "Decreases (1/R = 1/R1 + 1/R2 + ...)"
        ]
      ],
      "id": "jjn4eph"
    },
    {
      "id": "2mehn4d",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Circuit Type</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Description</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Series Circuit</p>",
            "<p>Components connected in a single path</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
